operator.faq.q001:
Creating a charging site on the platform requires completing the closed loop of “Basic Attributes - Business Parameters - Associated Bindings - Review & Publish”:
Basic attribute configuration: go to [Charger Management - Site Management - Create Station], and enter the station name, the operating entity (operator), the geographic location (latitude/longitude coordinates are used to position the station on the map), the total capacity of the distribution transformer (kVA), the open operating hours (24 hours / specified time windows), the parking fee description, and the site's supporting facilities (restroom, lounge, canopy, etc.);
Associated bindings: bind the corresponding billing template (vehicle billing / two-wheeler billing / occupancy billing) to the site, and divide the points and assign templates;

operator.faq.q002:
Full lifecycle device registration follows the logic “Model Management ➔ Device Records ➔ Connector Binding”:
Device model management: in [Device Management - Device Model], maintain the hardware vendor model library, and enter the device's electrical attributes (AC single-phase 7kW / DC dual-gun 120kW / liquid-cooled ultra-fast 480kW), output voltage and current range, protocol version (OCPP 1.6J/2.0.1 or the national standard protocol), and rated power;
Device record entry: in [Device Management], enter the unique identification serial number of the charger (SN code / device number) and the site it belongs to;
Connector mapping and QR code binding: for dual-gun or multi-gun devices, the system automatically generates an independent gun number code for each connector (e.g., connector 01, connector 02) and a corresponding client-side scan-to-start QR code, and supports batch export of printed charger-body material labels.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: when hardware devices are connected to the platform, the platform's unified WebSocket communication address and authentication key must be set at the underlying charger level; once the charger passes its network self-check, it will be shown as “Idle (Available)” in the backend [Charger Monitoring].

operator.faq.q004:
[Billing Management - Operation Billing Template] is responsible for modularly combining the grid catalog electricity price with the site's operational service fee, and supports rapidly rolling out one template to multiple sites:
24-hour time-slice segmentation: supports dividing a day's 24 hours into as many as 8～12 fine-grained periods, labeled respectively as [Critical Peak, On-Peak, Mid-Peak, Off-Peak, Super Off-Peak];
Dual-track billing separation: each period consists of two independent rate components:
[Base Electricity Price]: strictly anchored to the current commercial and industrial time-of-use catalog electricity price of the local Development and Reform Commission / grid company for the current period;
[Service Fee Rate]: the operator's operational service profit rate, supporting tiered pricing by time period (e.g., lowering the service fee during valley periods to attract ride-hailing vehicles to charge, and raising it during peak periods to balance grid load);
💡Business enablement and recommendations: when charging spans multiple periods, the system automatically slices the metered kWh at the time-of-use cut-off points and sums them segment by segment, ensuring that user bills and the operator's revenue data are 100% rigorously compliant.

operator.faq.q005:
Configure a scientific overtime occupancy fee mechanism through [Billing Management - Occupancy Billing Template], balancing vehicle owner experience with turnover rate:
Free grace period: usually recommended to be set to 15～30 minutes, reserving reasonable walking time for the vehicle owner to return and unplug after receiving the fully-charged notification;
Tiered and billing rates: supports per-minute billing (e.g., 0.5 CNY/min or 1.0 CNY/min, i.e., 30～60 CNY/hour);
Daily cap: to avoid exorbitant disputes under extreme circumstances, a maximum upper limit for the occupancy fee per order can be set (e.g., 100～200 CNY/day);
Applicable periods and station scope: supports enabling the occupancy fee during busy daytime hours (08:00～22:00); during the nighttime valley period (22:00～next day 08:00), the occupancy fee can be set to be waived, improving the satisfaction of vehicle owners who park and charge overnight.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: occupancy fee revenue is a separate line item in financial reconciliation, and can be statistically separated from regular electricity fees and service fees in backend reports, facilitating the calculation of dedicated revenue sharing with site partners.

operator.faq.q006:
[Charger Management - Device Whitelist] is used to let designated users or specific vehicles enjoy free or password-free charging at designated sites:
Applicable business scenarios:
[Station O&M and Engineering Testing]: providing test quota for on-site operations and maintenance technicians, avoiding actual charges during troubleshooting tests;
[Internal Employee and Executive Benefits]: for enterprises' internal self-use chargers, providing free commute charging for whitelisted employee vehicles;
[Core Strategic Partners]: exclusive free-of-charge benefits for partner automakers' test-drive vehicles and specific vehicles of site partners;

operator.faq.q007:
The platform adopts a mature multi-tenant / multi-operator architecture, supporting the main platform's unified enablement and independent accounting for franchise operators:
Operator onboarding review: after a franchise operator submits its business license, power access permit, and legal representative qualifications online, the main administrator performs onboarding review and account activation in the [Operations Management - Operators] module;
Asset and site ownership binding: clarifying the asset ownership of each charging station and each charger to an operator; a franchise operator logging into its independent sub-backend can only view and manage the sites, devices, orders, and revenue data under its own name, ensuring physical data isolation;
Operating rate and revenue-share ratio settings: setting revenue-sharing rules for the main platform and franchise operators (e.g., the platform charges a 5% software service fee and the franchise operator receives 95% of the electricity fee and service fee revenue); the system fully automatically calculates the split-payment ledger based on real-time orders.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: the main platform can, with one click, take in the aggregated data of the entire network, while granting franchise operators independent pricing rights and marketing coupon issuance permissions.

operator.faq.q008:
The platform provides franchise operators with a rigorous, transparent financial split and settlement system:
Real-time order accounting: after each charging order is completed, the system automatically breaks it down according to the preset revenue-sharing ratio into [Base Electricity Fee Attribution], [Operator Service Fee Sharing], and [Platform Technical Service Fee], and writes them into the clearing ledger;
Periodic reconciliation and bill confirmation: on a fixed monthly settlement date, the system automatically generates a monthly settlement summary for the franchise operator in [Finance Management - Reconciliation Bills], including actual charging volume, total transaction amount, refund offset items, and the net revenue share due;
Withdrawal application and multi-level review: the franchise operator initiates a [Wallet Balance Withdrawal Request] in its backend, and after the main platform's finance administrator verifies the invoicing qualifications and the reconciliation statement in [Finance Management - Withdrawal Review] and finds them correct, it clicks to approve, and the funds are paid to the operator's corporate account via corporate online banking or a third-party payment channel.
⚠️Operational risk-control reminder: before approving a withdrawal, it should be verified whether the operator has issued VAT special invoices for the corresponding amount to the platform in full, ensuring a compliant closed loop of financial and tax documents.

operator.faq.q009:
Multi-dimensional coupon configuration: create coupon assets in [Charger Management - Charging Coupons]:
[Denomination Type]: configure instant-discount coupons (e.g., CNY 5 off), discount coupons (e.g., 20% off the service fee), or threshold-discount coupons (spend CNY 30, get CNY 6 off);
[Usage Threshold and Time Period Restrictions]: can be restricted to valley periods only, achieving the goal of “shaving peaks and filling valleys and guiding vehicle owners to consume electricity during valley periods”;
[Applicable Station Scope]: can be designated for newly launched sites only, precisely channeling traffic to cold or new sites;
Issuance channels: supports users independently claiming coupons in the client, automatic coupon issuance on new user registration, and administrators performing targeted, precise manual coupon issuance to high-value vehicle owners in [Member List].
💡Business enablement and recommendations: coupons strictly follow a deduction order at settlement (usually the service fee is deducted first); for abnormal orders where no electricity was actually charged, the system automatically returns the coupon and extends its validity period.

operator.faq.q010:
The fleet module provides B2B-exclusive management capabilities for high-frequency, high-volume key-account groups:
Fleet organization and member registration: create a fleet record in [Charger Management - Fleet Management], entering the enterprise name, contact person, and binding a fleet-exclusive fund pool account, and batch-import the fleet's driver accounts and license plate numbers / vehicle VIN codes;
Fleet-exclusive billing strategy: supports binding an exclusive discounted billing template to different fleets (e.g., granting a logistics fleet 30% off the service fee); when a driver charges, the system automatically identifies the fleet identity and settles at the negotiated price;
Centralized reconciliation and quota control: the fleet supports unified recharge and prepayment via the enterprise master account; drivers' charging directly deducts from the master account quota, and at month-end the operator exports a fleet summary reconciliation statement and issues an enterprise VAT invoice.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: key-account fleets can provide a site with a stable guaranteed base load and predictable cash flow, and are a key pillar for improving equipment utilization at new sites.

operator.faq.q011:
[Finance Management - Charging Order Management] supports panoramic monitoring of vehicle charging, two-wheeler charging, occupancy fee orders, and abnormal orders:
Order status full-process tracking: records in real time the full-chain messages and data of an order from “Created ➔ Authentication Handshake ➔ Charging ➔ Settling ➔ Completed/Refunded” (including start SOC, end SOC, total charging kWh, segmented amounts by period, unplug time, etc.);
Abnormal order intelligent interception and classification: when the system detects the following abnormal events, it automatically marks them as [Pending Abnormal Orders] and triggers a to-do notification to the administrator:
[Zero-kWh Billing Anomaly]: the order is created and charged but the actual energy delivered is < 0.2 kWh;
[Offline Gun Trip Interruption]: a sudden network disconnection or hardware power loss at the charger causes no normal StopTransaction message to be received;
Abnormal handling actions: the administrator can retrieve the charger's original MeterValues messages online for verification, and perform [One-Click Refund to Original Payment Channel], [Manually Correct Settlement Amount], or [Void Order and Re-settle].
💡Business enablement and recommendations: for abnormal charges confirmed to be caused by equipment failure, after finance review approval the system will initiate a second-level refund back to the original payment method within 5 minutes, protecting the vehicle owner's lawful rights and interests.

operator.faq.q012:
Operator finance staff complete rigorous reconciliation of the three-party fund flow and business flow through [Finance Management - Reconciliation Bills]:
User top-up and consumption reconciliation: verify the total actual top-up transaction amount of users via WeChat / Alipay / bank card against the detailed changes in the platform account wallet balance ([Top-up Records] vs [Balance Details]), ensuring the books match reality;
Operator electricity fee and service fee gross margin accounting:
[Revenue Side]: export the [Base Electricity Fee Revenue] and [Service Fee Revenue] of all completed charging orders in the current period;
[Cost Side]: enter the total-meter electricity bill actually paid to the grid company (including the transformer basic capacity fee / demand charge and the electricity fee for the actual total kWh);
⚠️Operational risk-control reminder: if the deviation between the site's total meter reading and the sum of the output readings of all chargers exceeds 12%, one should immediately be alert to excessive cable line loss, meter failure, or hidden electricity theft, and dispatch an O&M team for on-site investigation.

operator.faq.q013:
[Finance Management - Invoice Management] fully satisfies the national fully-digitalized e-invoice and tax classification code specifications:
Business and tax rate separation mechanism: according to national tax policy, the electricity fee and service fee in a charging order belong to different tax categories:
[Electricity Fee Portion]: belongs to goods sales, with a VAT rate usually of 13%;
[Charging Service Fee Portion]: belongs to modern services, with a VAT rate usually of 6%; when a user applies for an invoice, the system automatically splits the selected order amount proportionally into two tax sub-items, "electricity fee" and "service fee";
Invoicing mode support: supports [User Self-Service Application ➔ Automatic Issuance via the Digital Invoice Tax-Control Interface ➔ Automatic Delivery to User's Email], and also supports finance administrators performing [Issued After Manual Review Confirmation] when enterprise key accounts apply for special invoices;
Invoicing quota anti-cash-out risk control: the system only allows invoices to be issued for “the cash-paid portion that has actually been consumed and settled”, and automatically excludes unconsumed top-up amounts, promotional gift funds, and coupon-deducted portions from the invoicing quota.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: through the backend invoicing ledger, operator finance can export the detailed output tax amount for the month with one click, directly usable for filing tax returns with the tax bureau.

operator.faq.q014:
[Smart Charging] is the core intelligent technology with which operators respond to insufficient grid distribution capacity, reduce demand charges, and prevent transformer overload:
Core operating strategy: under the premise that a site's total distribution transformer capacity is fixed, the platform dynamically issues power adjustment commands to each charger based on real-time grid load, time-of-use price signals, each vehicle's battery state of charge (SOC), and the vehicle owner's reserved departure time:
[Transformer Overload Protection]: when the total power of all vehicles plugged in and charging at a site simultaneously approaches the upper limit of the transformer's safe capacity (e.g., the 85% warning line), the system automatically performs millisecond-level dynamic derating and equal sharing of each connector's output power, preventing the main switch from tripping;
[Time-of-Use Price Economic Dispatch]: automatically schedules full-power, full-speed charging during valley price periods, and appropriately limits power during grid peak periods while meeting the vehicle owner's basic charging needs, helping reduce overall electricity costs;
[Urgency-First Algorithm]: the system prioritizes high-power charging for vehicles with extremely low battery (e.g., SOC < 20%), and releases the surplus power quota of vehicles already charged above 80% and entering the trickle stage to adjacent newly plugged-in vehicles;
Commercial value: operators can add 1.5～2 times as many charging points under existing distribution capacity without spending heavily to apply to the grid for transformer expansion, greatly improving the single-site construction ROI.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: orderly charging supports flexibly setting control rules and the transformer rated safety threshold per site in the backend [Special Features - Smart Charging Strategy].

operator.faq.q015:
The dashboard module is the data cockpit with which operators grasp business trends and formulate dynamic strategies:
[Homepage Data Overview] core metrics:
[Total Operating Revenue]: cumulative total revenue for today / this month, intuitively broken down into base electricity fee and service fee gross margin, comparing day-over-day and month-over-month increases or decreases;
[Charging Volume and Sessions]: network-wide total charging kWh (kWh), total vehicles served, and average charging duration per vehicle;
[Device Operating Health]: network-wide total number of devices, online rate, fault rate, idle rate, and the proportion currently charging;
[Station Revenue Ranking]: displays in real time the network-wide revenue Top 10 and the bottom-ranked site list, assisting in precisely locating high-quality assets and problem sites;
[Station Data Dashboard] fine-grained drill-down: using a single site as the micro dimension, displays the single site's daily average charging volume trend, per-connector daily output (CNY/connector/day), per-connector daily average vehicles served, and user recharge retention rate, directly reflecting that site's business health.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: at the daily morning meeting the operations team can focus on reviewing yesterday's revenue fluctuations and the proportion of faulty chargers, and promptly launch targeted valley-period discount coupon campaigns for low-efficiency sites.

operator.faq.q017:
[Special Features - Data Statistics] provides comprehensive statistical reports with multiple perspectives, exportability, and multi-level drill-down:
Operator statistics: compare the asset scale, charging kWh, fault response timeliness, and platform revenue contribution of each franchise operator to evaluate the effectiveness of cooperation;
Station statistics: by region and site type, statistics on station return on investment period (ROI), average revenue per kWh, and revenue per unit area;
Device statistics: by hardware vendor and model, statistics on device failure frequency (MTBF), average repair time, and communication disconnection rate, providing hard assessment criteria for subsequent device tendering and supplier elimination;
User and vehicle statistics: statistics on the number of newly registered users, active charging users, and customer unit price distribution, as well as the distribution of charging vehicle brands and battery capacities, assisting analysis of mainstream vehicle owners' charging profiles and electricity usage habits.
💡Business enablement and recommendations: all statistical reports support one-click generation by day, week, month, or custom time range and export to Excel, facilitating reporting to finance and management.
